WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:05.470
If I told you that you were 103 pages and
less than 2 hours away from improving your

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.709
speaking skills, would you believe me?

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:08.730
Well, keep watching!

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.650
Today, we are going to talk about a short-but-excellent
book that will immediately supercharge your

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.629
speaking skills in a foreign language.

00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:17.590
Hey friends, welcome back to my channel.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:21.870
For those who don't know me yet, I am Luca
Lampariello, a language coach who speaks 14

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:22.870
languages.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.689
On this channel, I help people learn foreign
languages more efficiently, faster and in

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:29.150
a fun way
Confess to it.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.460
Ever since you decided to learn a foreign
language, you’ve been looking for that quick

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:33.460
fix.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:34.800
You know, the shortcut.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.190
The thing that gets you fluent, faster.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:38.530
It’s not just you.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.260
Everybody’s looking for the same thing.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.309
That’s why everywhere you look, you can
find tips and methods to learn a language

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:48.719
in a year, a month, a week, a day, and even
in a matter of hours and minutes.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:51.660
51’’-56’’ ⇒ to be cut
I hate to be the party pooper but let me tell

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.160
you straight away that these “magic pills”
don’t exist.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.600
If you start learning a language tomorrow,
you’ll still be learning it by next weekend,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.821
next February, and the next time Halley’s
comet passes by — unless, of course, you

00:01:04.821 --> 00:01:06.890
choose not to maintain it.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.930
So, you can’t improve your knowledge of
a language immediately.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.300
That is out of our reach.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.920
But, if you’re looking for ways to improve
your foreign language skills immediately,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.700
those, my friends, are quite easily available.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.170
And you can put them to use in your life much
faster than you think, thanks to a little

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:32.050
book called How to Improve Your Foreign Language
Immediately, by Boris Shekhtman.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.670
Before we dig into our review of the book,
let me present a simple metaphor:

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:36.670
Clothes.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:37.670
I have them.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:38.670
You have them.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:39.670
We all have them.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:44.340
Right now, at this exact moment in time, you
have a finite amount of clothing that is yours

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.180
to use as you wish.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.760
Unless you want to wear all of your clothing
at once, you need a place to store it all.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.470
One option would be to keep it all in a pile.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:58.860
You know—a big, tangled, mess of socks,
sweaters, scarves, t-shirts, and tank tops

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.270
all gathered together in one corner of your
bedroom.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:05.380
Another option would be to separate and organize
it all.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.570
You can hang the shirts, scarves, and tank
tops in your closet, and fold the scarves

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:13.810
and pair the socks for safekeeping deep in
your dresser drawers.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:19.020
You can even organize each individual item
by color, or season in which you wear it.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.840
Now, I’m sure you’ll agree that in both
scenarios, you have the same amount of clothing.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.330
Having a closet doesn’t make clothes magically
appear, or disappear.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:32.450
But let me ask you this:
If I gave you ten seconds to find a specific

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:38.210
pair of socks, in which scenario would you
be most likely to succeed?

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:39.550
The second, of course.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:44.200
Having organizational tools like a closet
or set of drawers will give you a higher chance

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.180
of being able to find and use the clothes
you need, exactly when you need them.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.800
This is exactly what How to Improve Your Foreign
Language Immediately provides.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.540
It doesn’t give you more language knowledge.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.540
It doesn’t teach you nouns, adjectives,
politeness forms, or how to better use the

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:58.670
subjunctive mood.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:03.440
Instead, it gives you a set of seven intuitive
tools that help you organize and make better

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.620
use of the knowledge you already have, no
matter how minuscule — or expansive — that

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.360
knowledge may be.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.210
The book, in short, teaches you to use language
effectively.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.410
Let’s dig in to our review.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.680
How to Improve Your Foreign Language Immediately,
by Boris Shekhtman is a print book, the most

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.380
current edition of which was released in 2013.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:27.750
The book, a short read at 103 pages, is divided
into 9 separate chapters — one introductory

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.930
chapter, seven body chapters, and an epilogue.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:35.350
Each of the seven body chapters is devoted
to a particular “communicative tool”.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.790
In the author’s own words:
“Communicative tools are the combination

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:44.840
of skills which allow a speaker to use most
effectively the level of foreign language

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.319
in his or her command”
The chapters dedicated to these effective

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:52.350
speaking strategies generally contain the
following:

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:57.070
A description of the tool
An example of the tool in practice (typically

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:01.120
through a hypothetical conversation between
a foreign speaker and a native)

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.590
An analysis of the example(s)
An exercise through which to practice the

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.630
tool
In addition, sprinkled throughout the book

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.890
are a series of anecdotes — personal stories
taken from Shekhtman’s experiences teaching

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:19.590
Russian to English speakers, either one-on-one
or in a small-group, classroom setting.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.531
Considering that 75% of this short, simple
book is devoted solely to teaching you to

00:04:23.531 --> 00:04:28.550
use those tools right away, you get a lot
of value in a compact, inexpensive package.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:32.340
Of course, none of that information would
matter if the tools weren’t so useful and

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.340
intuitive.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:38.130
Each tool can be immediately put to use in
one of three specific areas:

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:43.550
Quantity/quality of communication (Show Your
Stuff, Build Up Your Islands)

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:49.120
Communication in difficult scenarios (Shifting
Gears, Simplify, Simplify; Breaking Away)

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.340
Increasing expressiveness of language (Embellish
it, Say What?)

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:59.180
These areas are ones that all language learners
seek to improve, regardless of target language

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.160
and target language proficiency.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.990
As bizarre as it seems, as you read up on
each of Shekhtman’s seven tools, you’ll

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:10.040
immediately understand how they’ll make
you a better speaker of your target language.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.010
How can that be?

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:16.350
Because, knowingly or not, you’ve already
used these tools to communicate better in

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.480
your own native language.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.650
You may even use several (or all) of them
every day.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:23.650
Think about it.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:29.370
Have you ever told a joke so many times to
so many people that, by the umpteenth telling,

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:34.470
you know exactly what words, what pauses,
what intonation, and what facial expressions

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.830
to get the maximum number of laughs from your
audience?

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:43.020
That joke, according to Shekhtman, is a well-practiced
"island" that has been built-up and refined

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.389
according to Tool #2, Build Your Islands.

00:05:46.389 --> 00:05:50.580
Have you ever been chatting with a new group
of people when, all of a sudden, talk shifts

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.800
to a subject you know nothing about?

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.840
Instead of pretending you know about a field
you clearly don’t, you use some conversational

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:03.090
kung-fu to coax the topic of conversation
towards some common ground, so you can resume

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.729
speaking freely again.

00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:08.710
The act of deftly changing the topic when
we’re out of our conversational depth is

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:14.330
what Shekhtman would call Shifting Gears,
and is the third tool that the book provides.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:18.180
Have you ever tried to explain a difficult
concept to a friend or family member and,

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.700
despite your efforts, been completely unsuccessful?

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:26.979
Instead of giving up however, you choose to
simplify your words and phrasing, and try

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:27.979
again?

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:32.910
Simplifying complex language is an example
of Shekhtman’s fourth tool, Simplify, Simplify,

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:37.770
and is just as useful amongst native speakers
as it is between natives and foreigners.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:38.770
See what I mean?

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:43.390
You don’t have to have tried out all of
these tools in a language learning context

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:45.900
to know that they’ll help you communicate
better.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.400
Because, in the end, they’re not language
learning tools.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.180
They’re communication tools.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.000
And no matter how old you are, if you’re
reading this, you’ve done enough communication

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.130
already to have an intuitive feel for what
works and doesn’t work.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:02.860
All of Shekhtman’s seven tools have this
intuitive quality.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:06.479
As far as I am concerned,I have been using
a lot the island part.

00:07:06.479 --> 00:07:09.400
I normally build islands that I work and rework.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:14.389
For each island, I built a set of 30 sentences,
I practice with my tutor and then I write

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:17.460
everything into my notebook and review them.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.150
Occasionally I practice them in real-life.

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:20.150
They come quite in-handy!

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:21.630
How to Improve Your Foreign Language Immediately's
communicative tools shine as intuitive guidelines

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:22.630
for improving language fluency right now.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:26.150
With the advice in the book, you have everything
you need to communicate your “current”

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:31.430
level, navigate difficult conversations with
natives, and speak in a more fluid, natural

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:32.430
way.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:38.000
Given the book’s short, compact form, these
tools are easy to practice and review on the

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.000
fly.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.850
I highly recommend this book to any learner,
of any language, at any level of skill.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:47.990
In terms of actionable skills, this book provides
most bang for its buck of any language learning

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:49.400
book on the market.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:55.120
I only wish that the book were of higher quality,
with more substantial references and background

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:56.250
material.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:00.639
So here is my suggestion for you guys:
Get the book (check link in the description

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:03.449
Box)
Read it in a week (it is short, but you might

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:06.949
want to use it as a guide)
Underline the parts that are relevant and

00:08:06.949 --> 00:08:10.280
interesting for you
Make a list of activities you want to try

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:12.449
Apply what you've learned!

00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:15.820
The best way to learn is to do, not just read.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:22.389
And last but not least, jot down in a nice
lengthy comment below about your experience,

00:08:22.389 --> 00:08:25.220
your thoughts about the book, what worked
and what didn't.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:29.680
Doing and sharing are the best ways to stay
motivated and take things to the next level

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:30.840
Last but not least, if you liked this video,
you'll love this other video I have made about

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:31.979
how long it takes to Learn a language to Fluency
and you can click here.

00:08:31.979 --> 00:08:33.930
As always, thanks for watching, and see you
in the next video!

